# Python-generated files
__pycache__/
*.py[oc]
build/
dist/
wheels/
*.egg-info

# Virtual environments
.venv

# Secrets / environment
.env

# Local agent instructions — kept out of the public repo
CLAUDE.md

# Generated artifacts (regenerate via ingest / answer time)
qdrant_data/
page_images/

# Fetched eval-corpus PDFs (regenerate: scripts/fetch_eval_corpus.py). The three demo
# documents stay tracked; everything else in pdfs/ is the distractor haystack, pinned by
# sha256 in eval/corpus_manifest.json rather than committed (~200 MB of papers).
pdfs/*.pdf
!pdfs/sales_report.pdf
!pdfs/attention.pdf
!pdfs/colpali.pdf

# Eval reports (regenerate via eval/run_eval.py; the dataset is tracked). The pinned
# baseline is the exception - a regression guard needs a committed thing to diff against.
# Note `reports/*`, not `reports/`: git never descends into an excluded *directory*, so
# the trailing-slash form would make the negation below unreachable.
eval/reports/*
# The current baseline is `baseline_decomposed.json` (83 questions at the shipped defaults:
# RETRIEVE_K=12, MAX_PAGES_PER_DOC=5, RERANK_K=3, QUERY_DECOMPOSE=true,
# DECOMPOSE_ORIGINAL_WEIGHT=0). The other five are superseded but kept because the write-ups
# cite their numbers: `baseline_diverse.json` is the same questions with decomposition off,
# and is the correct thing to diff the query-decomposition pass against; `baseline_swept.json`
# is them at RETRIEVE_K=10 with no cap, which the slate-diversity pass was measured against;
# `baseline_rerank_k3.json` is that config on the pre-sweep labels; `baseline_sharpened.json`
# is those 83 questions at RERANK_K=2, and doubles as arm A of the RERANK_K experiment;
# `baseline_desaturated.json` is the older 69-question set.
!eval/reports/baseline_decomposed.json
!eval/reports/baseline_diverse.json
!eval/reports/baseline_swept.json
!eval/reports/baseline_rerank_k3.json
!eval/reports/baseline_sharpened.json
!eval/reports/baseline_desaturated.json
# Arms B and C of the RERANK_K experiment, kept so the decision stays auditable at the
# row level rather than only through the summary table in PRODUCTION_HARDENING.md - a
# re-run cannot reproduce them (the answer step is not deterministic). They are scored
# against the PRE-SWEEP gold labels and predate `candidate_pages`, so they cannot be
# re-scored offline either: compare them only with each other and with
# `baseline_rerank_k3.json`, never with `baseline_swept.json`. Nothing in the harness or
# the tests reads them.
!eval/reports/arm_b_k3.json
!eval/reports/arm_c_k3_adaptive.json
# The k=50 retrieval probe, kept for the opposite reason to the arms above: it is perfectly
# reproducible, but it is a *simulator*. With 50 candidates per row stored in
# `candidate_pages`, and gold_rank/both coverages being pure functions of (pages, gold), any
# slate policy can be scored against it offline with no model, no Qdrant and no API key - the
# slate-diversity pass chose its cap, its fanout depth and its two rejected designs that way
# before writing code. Regenerating it needs the full corpus ingested and the ~2B model
# loaded, which a fresh clone does not have. The slate-diversity arms themselves are NOT kept:
# they are deterministic and re-run from a one-line prefix.
!eval/reports/probe_k50_retrieval.json
# The visual-token arm and its control (retrieval-only), committed for the same reason the
# RERANK_K arms above are: the ingest-throughput pass write-up cites their numbers, and the
# 512 arm is the evidence for a *rejection* - which is the easiest kind of result to lose.
# `vt_control.json` doubles as the proof that pass changed no vectors: it re-scored the
# pinned index under the new code and reproduced baseline_decomposed.json to four decimals.
!eval/reports/vt_control.json
!eval/reports/vt512.json

# Ingest profiles (regenerate via scripts/profile_ingest.py). Same `reports/*`-not-`reports/`
# form as above, for the same reason: git will not descend into an excluded directory.
bench/reports/*
# The pinned profile, kept because src/config.py and src/embedder.py cite its numbers - the
# 98% embed share is what justifies batching the forward pass and ignoring every other stage.
# Its arms are all DEGENERATE on purpose: it was measured on MPS + bfloat16, where the
# MPS batching exclusion forces single-page forward passes, so every arm reports
# `effective_batch_size: 1` regardless of what it requested. That makes it evidence of two
# things and not a third - it backs the 98% share and it records what an MPS ingest actually
# does, but it is NOT a measurement of what batching buys. Four arms doing identical
# batch-1 work spread 8.242 -> 12.074 s/page here, so it also pins this box's noise floor at
# ~46%: anything under a 1.5x claim is unmeasurable on it. A CUDA run would supersede it.
!bench/reports/ingest_baseline.json
# NOT a baseline, and stamped `measurement_quality: CONTENDED` in its own config block. It is
# the only run in the new preprocess/forward/decode + upsert format, kept so the added fields
# have a worked example. Do not read its `preprocess` as contention: preprocess runs on the
# prefetch thread now, so GIL contention stretches its wall time to ~0.9-1.2s against the
# 0.304s it cost serially - that gap is the overlap working, not a noisy box. What does make
# it unpinnable is the spread: arms doing identical batch-1 work measured 4.96 to 17.12 s/page
# across attempts. Re-pin ingest_baseline.json from a genuinely quiet box.
!bench/reports/ingest_contended.json
